**Lemma 3.2 (control origin and failure exclusion).** On the fixed
canonical graph, an actual occurrence of $E_0$ is the Image-success arm
of an authentic accepted-target flight. Starting there and stopping at
its subsequent $E_1$ without leaving this stage follows RenderS,
Publish0 and CheckS0 of that same flight. No Rejection, callback-fault,
other View stage, final Image-value check or bank-encoded simulated PC
can supply either success handoff as that stage occurrence.

Proof. GENERAL Definition 2.1 expands fixed calls with fresh temporaries
and physical labels before numbering. Finite parity alternatives retain
that call-site distinction. Its complete successful table, Definition
7.2, and CYCLE Definition 8.3 give the following incoming and outgoing
joins for the interval.

| Physical region | Incoming success join and next successful region |
| --- | --- |
| RenderS | ClientImage's actual Image-success handoff $E_0$; then this call site's Publish0. |
| Publish0 | This RenderS after constructing S and clearing Image work; then the actual handler→client TOK/CTL to CheckS0. |
| CheckS0 | This Publish0 after complete receiver print; then its full check/ACK/return and $E_1$ to FromS. |

Within a region, the only extra edges are its expanded helper sequence,
finite tests and local while back edges. Hygienic inlining does not create
a return from a distinct call site into this one. All static stage joins
are the displayed ones. The later CheckS1 uses a separate inlined
CheckImageValue subgraph and exits to FinalReturn, not RenderS. Its
represented argument, and all represented nested PCs, are bank data;
GCA Definition 4.1 and GENERAL's physical numbering keep them separate
from the actual outer PC.

There are three relevant failure exits. ARC Definition 7.3/GCA Definition
6.3's handler failure publishes kind0011 Rejection and enters the
ClientImage rejection arm, which checks reason/request and returns a
failed cycle to Ready. Its Image codec/binding refusal appends a fault,
ACKs and returns a failed cycle. CYCLE Definition 8.5/GENERAL Definition
7.4's new handler failure likewise takes Rejection; a new callback failure
already holding a receiver publishes nothing, records its fault and
returns a failed cycle. None of these arms has the success CTL into
RenderS or the success CTL into FromS. Their occurrence of shared cleanup
operations does not identify their distinct final successors. A disabled
operation supplies no executed edge.

Every canonical acquired flight starts from its actual preceding Ready;
no busy second entry exists. Before Image success, the preserved
Recover/ClientCover/Bind/ClientIssue/Call route resolves the actual
immutable cover/package and checks the selected target. GENERAL Lemma
5.3 applies to that accepted target. Thus an actual $E_0$ on this domain
is reached through that route. The listed stage edges, absence of external
input or scheduling between them, and the enumerated failure exits prove
the asserted interval and exclusions. This is a check of this specified
finite construction, not an assumption that arbitrary successful-looking
output authenticates its producer. ∎

